1
00:00:00,750 --> 00:00:03,810
A one-word correction doesn't need to become a

2
00:00:03,810 --> 00:00:06,750
space mission, but when a change involves reading

3
00:00:06,750 --> 00:00:09,630
code, updating tests, and running checks,

4
00:00:10,310 --> 00:00:11,910
coordinating that work can be useful.

5
00:00:12,630 --> 00:00:15,870
In this lesson, we'll compare direct editing, Inline

6
00:00:15,870 --> 00:00:18,750
Edit, and Agent using real work in our

7
00:00:18,750 --> 00:00:19,730
practice repository.

8
00:00:20,470 --> 00:00:22,550
The goal is to choose a tool for

9
00:00:22,550 --> 00:00:25,250
a reason you can explain, then verify the

10
00:00:25,250 --> 00:00:28,050
result with the same care, whichever route you take.

11
00:00:30,517 --> 00:00:32,697
Start with three kinds of effort,

12
00:00:33,737 --> 00:00:37,077
understanding the task, making the
change, and checking the result.

13
00:00:37,857 --> 00:00:40,517
If you already know the exact edit, direct

14
00:00:40,517 --> 00:00:41,677
editing may be quickest.

15
00:00:42,477 --> 00:00:44,757
If you can describe a transformation within a

16
00:00:44,757 --> 00:00:47,177
selected range, Inline Edit can help.

17
00:00:47,837 --> 00:00:51,657
If exploration, file changes,
and commands need coordination,

18
00:00:52,217 --> 00:00:52,917
consider Agent.

19
00:00:53,637 --> 00:00:55,497
These aren't rigid categories.

20
00:00:55,497 --> 00:00:59,577
The useful question is where
assistance reduces effort

21
00:00:59,577 --> 00:01:02,197
without making the result harder to understand.

22
00:01:04,783 --> 00:01:08,263
Here's a deliberately prepared
typo in the contract comment.

23
00:01:08,983 --> 00:01:10,043
Labels is misspelled.

24
00:01:10,683 --> 00:01:12,723
We know the replacement and we aren't changing

25
00:01:12,723 --> 00:01:14,203
the behavior of either helper.

26
00:01:14,543 --> 00:01:17,323
So select the word, type the correction, and save.

27
00:01:17,983 --> 00:01:19,363
That's a complete tool choice.

28
00:01:19,743 --> 00:01:21,603
The editor already gives us what we need.

29
00:01:22,083 --> 00:01:23,643
You can still use AI for a correction

30
00:01:23,643 --> 00:01:25,443
like this but there should be some benefit

31
00:01:25,443 --> 00:01:26,883
beyond using the newest button.

32
00:01:29,183 --> 00:01:32,143
The Git diff shows one comment correction.

33
00:01:32,783 --> 00:01:34,563
The function bodies haven't changed.

34
00:01:35,143 --> 00:01:37,403
This is the kind of evidence appropriate to

35
00:01:37,403 --> 00:01:38,923
a spelling fix in a comment.

36
00:01:39,443 --> 00:01:42,223
It doesn't require inventing a new behavior test.

37
00:01:42,903 --> 00:01:44,083
Keep that distinction in mind.

38
00:01:44,863 --> 00:01:48,083
Verification should respond to
what could actually be affected.

39
00:01:48,683 --> 00:01:50,243
We'll leave this small edit in the working

40
00:01:50,243 --> 00:01:52,843
tree and explicitly tell the
next tool to preserve it.

41
00:01:55,050 --> 00:01:57,310
This is footage from the previous lesson.

42
00:01:57,950 --> 00:02:01,250
We selected the display helper, described one exact

43
00:02:01,250 --> 00:02:03,950
label change, and reviewed the resulting proposal.

44
00:02:04,810 --> 00:02:07,010
Inline Edit was a useful fit because the

45
00:02:07,010 --> 00:02:09,650
target was known and the transformation was bounded.

46
00:02:10,610 --> 00:02:12,630
Direct editing would also have been reasonable if

47
00:02:12,630 --> 00:02:14,110
we already knew the implementation.

48
00:02:15,030 --> 00:02:17,670
Choosing Inline Edit didn't remove the need to

49
00:02:17,670 --> 00:02:20,730
review the code or run the
existing behavioral checks.

50
00:02:23,217 --> 00:02:25,497
Now the task has several related pieces.

51
00:02:25,937 --> 00:02:27,797
We want a helper that converts a list

52
00:02:27,797 --> 00:02:30,017
of status values into display labels.

53
00:02:30,377 --> 00:02:32,437
Reuse the existing scalar helper.

54
00:02:32,877 --> 00:02:35,177
Preserve the input order and repeated values.

55
00:02:35,757 --> 00:02:37,637
Return an empty list for empty input.

56
00:02:38,117 --> 00:02:39,457
And leave the input untouched.

57
00:02:40,117 --> 00:02:42,057
We'll also need tests for those requirements.

58
00:02:42,517 --> 00:02:44,097
This is a small example where we can

59
00:02:44,097 --> 00:02:46,597
inspect all the coordination before applying the same

60
00:02:46,597 --> 00:02:47,917
habits to larger features.

61
00:02:50,283 --> 00:02:53,043
In the Agent panel, name the new helper,

62
00:02:53,383 --> 00:02:56,183
its behavior, and the two files that may change.

63
00:02:56,943 --> 00:03:00,403
Ask for tests covering mixed
values, duplicates, empty

64
00:03:00,403 --> 00:03:02,043
input, and a frozen array.

65
00:03:02,803 --> 00:03:06,463
Preserve the existing labels, permission
policy, and comment correction.

66
00:03:07,243 --> 00:03:09,603
We also specify the commands to run and

67
00:03:09,603 --> 00:03:11,023
exclude unrelated work.

68
00:03:11,663 --> 00:03:14,643
The instruction gives the Agent useful room to

69
00:03:14,643 --> 00:03:17,703
implement the task while keeping the expected result

70
00:03:17,703 --> 00:03:19,423
and the review scope concrete.

71
00:03:21,983 --> 00:03:25,283
Submit the request and watch the actual workflow.

72
00:03:25,923 --> 00:03:29,243
Here, the Agent reads code,
changes the implementation

73
00:03:29,243 --> 00:03:31,403
and tests, and runs commands.

74
00:03:31,803 --> 00:03:34,263
That's the coordination we're asking it to handle.

75
00:03:34,703 --> 00:03:37,363
The exact sequence and waiting time can vary.

76
00:03:37,863 --> 00:03:39,263
Keep an eye on what it's doing.

77
00:03:39,643 --> 00:03:43,123
A clear request still needs
supervision appropriate to the work.

78
00:03:43,783 --> 00:03:46,963
For this exercise, everything stays in our dedicated

79
00:03:46,963 --> 00:03:50,083
practice checkout, and the task involves only the

80
00:03:50,083 --> 00:03:51,283
two named files.

81
00:03:53,750 --> 00:03:56,870
The Agent reports which files it modified and

82
00:03:56,870 --> 00:03:57,810
the checks it ran.

83
00:03:58,510 --> 00:04:00,410
That's useful as a guide to the review,

84
00:04:00,750 --> 00:04:02,950
but the report isn't our only evidence.

85
00:04:03,710 --> 00:04:06,210
Open the actual files and inspect the behavior.

86
00:04:07,150 --> 00:04:09,930
Notice that the earlier spelling fix is mentioned

87
00:04:09,930 --> 00:04:11,550
separately from the new helper.

88
00:04:12,230 --> 00:04:14,990
Keeping track of earlier
working tree changes matters.

89
00:04:15,510 --> 00:04:18,930
Otherwise, you can accidentally
attribute every change in

90
00:04:18,930 --> 00:04:21,870
the final diff to the most recent Agent request.

91
00:04:24,550 --> 00:04:27,230
The new helper maps each input through the

92
00:04:27,230 --> 00:04:29,090
existing scalar label function.

93
00:04:29,470 --> 00:04:32,770
That reuses our exact matching
and unknown label behavior.

94
00:04:33,430 --> 00:04:35,430
There is no need to duplicate the label

95
00:04:35,430 --> 00:04:37,470
branches or change the permission helper.

96
00:04:38,090 --> 00:04:40,350
Read the implementation against each requirement,

97
00:04:41,190 --> 00:04:44,130
order, duplicates, empty input, and no mutation.

98
00:04:44,990 --> 00:04:48,250
This implementation is short
enough to understand directly.

99
00:04:48,970 --> 00:04:51,910
Using Agent doesn't mean we should stop understanding

100
00:04:51,910 --> 00:04:53,210
the code it leaves behind.

101
00:04:55,750 --> 00:05:00,270
The new tests cover mixed exact and unknown values,

102
00:05:00,950 --> 00:05:04,690
repeated values, an empty array, and a frozen input.

103
00:05:05,230 --> 00:05:08,370
Look at the expected arrays, not just the test names.

104
00:05:09,110 --> 00:05:12,050
Do those expectations match
the behavior we requested?

105
00:05:12,610 --> 00:05:15,810
The frozen input case checks both the returned

106
00:05:15,810 --> 00:05:17,490
labels and the original array.

107
00:05:18,210 --> 00:05:21,470
When the same Agent writes implementation and tests,

108
00:05:21,870 --> 00:05:25,870
compare both with the independent
requirement. Matching mistakes

109
00:05:25,870 --> 00:05:29,110
in both files can still produce a green test run.

110
00:05:31,617 --> 00:05:35,157
After reviewing the
implementation and tests, keep the

111
00:05:35,157 --> 00:05:37,517
changes and inspect the repository diff.

112
00:05:38,097 --> 00:05:39,817
There are two modified files.

113
00:05:40,357 --> 00:05:43,237
The source contains our
earlier comment correction and

114
00:05:43,237 --> 00:05:44,237
the new batch helper.

115
00:05:44,777 --> 00:05:47,817
The test file imports that
helper and adds the new cases.

116
00:05:48,517 --> 00:05:51,137
Keeping the Agent's changes does not create a commit.

117
00:05:51,877 --> 00:05:53,997
The diff still needs to tell a coherent

118
00:05:53,997 --> 00:05:57,297
story, including work that was already present before

119
00:05:57,297 --> 00:05:58,117
the Agent started.

120
00:06:00,683 --> 00:06:03,103
Run the status test file in the terminal,

121
00:06:03,443 --> 00:06:04,983
then run the TypeScript check.

122
00:06:05,623 --> 00:06:09,123
All nine tests pass, five existing cases and

123
00:06:09,123 --> 00:06:10,023
four new cases.

124
00:06:10,703 --> 00:06:12,703
The type check also completes successfully.

125
00:06:13,343 --> 00:06:16,203
This verifies the bounded
helper work we just inspected.

126
00:06:16,783 --> 00:06:19,523
It doesn't demonstrate a live application screen or

127
00:06:19,523 --> 00:06:21,083
a complete authorization system.

128
00:06:21,463 --> 00:06:24,323
The value of the Agent's coordination is now

129
00:06:24,323 --> 00:06:26,443
connected to code and results that we can

130
00:06:26,443 --> 00:06:27,263
examine ourselves.

131
00:06:29,750 --> 00:06:31,650
Try the decision exercise.

132
00:06:32,390 --> 00:06:34,550
A known typo favors direct editing.

133
00:06:35,230 --> 00:06:38,390
A selected helper transformation can fit Inline Edit.

134
00:06:39,010 --> 00:06:42,130
A coordinated implementation and test change can fit

135
00:06:42,130 --> 00:06:45,290
Agent, although direct editing remains valid if you

136
00:06:45,290 --> 00:06:46,350
already know the work.

137
00:06:47,090 --> 00:06:49,630
Then look at the unclear permission request.

138
00:06:50,270 --> 00:06:53,130
No editing tool can supply the missing product

139
00:06:53,130 --> 00:06:54,370
decision responsibly.

140
00:06:54,990 --> 00:06:57,350
Investigate the intended policy first.

141
00:06:57,350 --> 00:07:01,010
File count alone doesn't tell
you which route is sensible.

142
00:07:03,517 --> 00:07:06,837
Use this original expected value model to think

143
00:07:06,837 --> 00:07:08,317
through the batch contract.

144
00:07:09,177 --> 00:07:11,237
Mixed values preserve their positions.

145
00:07:12,017 --> 00:07:13,557
Duplicates stay duplicated.

146
00:07:14,257 --> 00:07:16,437
Empty input produces an empty result.

147
00:07:17,237 --> 00:07:19,497
You can enter your own array and calculate

148
00:07:19,497 --> 00:07:20,717
the expected labels.

149
00:07:21,317 --> 00:07:23,697
This page doesn't run the repository code.

150
00:07:24,437 --> 00:07:28,037
It helps you state expectations before comparing them

151
00:07:28,037 --> 00:07:29,137
with the real tests.

152
00:07:29,957 --> 00:07:33,417
Check input immutability in
the actual checkout, using

153
00:07:33,417 --> 00:07:35,917
a frozen array as one concrete case.

154
00:07:38,417 --> 00:07:40,457
Save a short decision record.

155
00:07:40,917 --> 00:07:45,057
The starting revision, desired
behavior, chosen tool, changed

156
00:07:45,057 --> 00:07:46,997
files, and the checks you observed.

157
00:07:47,757 --> 00:07:49,817
Explain what work the tool helped with.

158
00:07:50,397 --> 00:07:53,097
In this example, we made the
known correction directly.

159
00:07:53,737 --> 00:07:57,457
Then, Agent coordinated
implementation, tests, and commands.

160
00:07:58,137 --> 00:08:02,097
Another engineer might reasonably
make the whole change directly.

161
00:08:02,097 --> 00:08:05,297
A useful record explains the choice and the

162
00:08:05,297 --> 00:08:08,877
result without pretending there
is only one correct workflow.

163
00:08:11,383 --> 00:08:14,443
For practice, add a batch helper that reuses

164
00:08:14,443 --> 00:08:16,643
the existing approval decision function.

165
00:08:17,223 --> 00:08:20,203
Keep the role policy unchanged and preserve order,

166
00:08:20,583 --> 00:08:23,303
duplicates, empty input, and the original array.

167
00:08:24,043 --> 00:08:25,923
Choose your own route and justify it.

168
00:08:26,483 --> 00:08:29,443
Inspect the implementation
and tests, run the relevant

169
00:08:29,443 --> 00:08:32,043
checks, and record one limit of your evidence.

170
00:08:32,743 --> 00:08:36,043
The exercise is about choosing
assistance deliberately.

171
00:08:36,043 --> 00:08:39,163
You don't need to reproduce our exact sequence

172
00:08:39,163 --> 00:08:41,163
of buttons to demonstrate that skill.

173
00:08:43,650 --> 00:08:46,130
You now have a practical way to choose

174
00:08:46,130 --> 00:08:49,370
among direct editing, Inline Edit, and Agent.

175
00:08:50,050 --> 00:08:53,090
Ask what you already understand,
what needs coordination,

176
00:08:53,570 --> 00:08:55,390
and how you'll verify the outcome.

177
00:08:56,070 --> 00:08:58,310
A small known edit can stay small.

178
00:08:58,870 --> 00:09:01,250
A broader task can benefit from an Agent

179
00:09:01,250 --> 00:09:03,610
without losing a concrete review boundary.

180
00:09:04,370 --> 00:09:07,450
Before moving on, name one task you'd edit

181
00:09:07,450 --> 00:09:10,930
directly, and one where Agent would save meaningful

182
00:09:10,930 --> 00:09:12,010
coordination work.

183
00:09:12,510 --> 00:09:15,530
Explain the difference in the
work, not just the tool names.
